## 3. Locked Architecture Decisions

### No-server rule (hard constraint)
Xury never hardcodes, embeds, or defaults to any server address —
not even public STUN. Every traversal target is caller-supplied
(`ctx->peer`). See PROBING_DESIGN.md.

### Layer dependency rules (DEPENDENCY.md)
- A layer depends only on layers strictly below it.
- `api/` is two different things in one folder: foundational files
  (types.c, err.c, weapon.c, config.c, hooks.c, version.c — anyone
  may depend on these) vs orchestration-wrapper files (scan.c,
  engine.c, peer.c — not built, nothing may depend on these, they
  wrap everything else).
- weapons/ may use: core/, platform/, foundational api/, scan/math.h,
  analysis/ results, and exactly one Phase I file (peer/mirror.c)
  when needed (hole.c only) — never pull in relay.c/upgrade.c.

### F.3 math/classification (RESEARCH.md §6)
- Variance is computed over **delta** (step between consecutive
  ports), not raw port values — magnitude-independent.
- Step magnitude uses **median** of deltas, not least-squares slope
  (slope was too sensitive to single-outlier repeats).
- Direction (for predict_next) uses **majority vote** of signed
  deltas, not slope sign or first-delta sign.
- F.3c (score.c, 11 weapon-probability functions) returns
  `XURY_ERR_NOT_CALIBRATED` honestly — no fabricated formulas until
  real measurement data exists.

### smart/ module boundaries
- No shared `xury_smart_ctx_t` god-struct. cache.c and early_term.c
  are stateless (explicit typed params). learning.c is the only
  stateful module, owns its own fixed-size `xury_smart_learning_t`
  (XURY_NAT_COUNT × XURY_WEAPON_COUNT counters, no heap alloc).
- Scope strictly `(nat_type, weapon) -> {success, fail}`. Never grows
  into latency/cost/bandwidth models.
- adaptive.c (Stage 2) is a **recommender only** — peers with blitz,
  never a hard dependency. blitz/race.c must work fully with
  learning=NULL, falling back to api/weapon.c's locked priority
  order.

### Weapon priority (api/weapon.c, locked, tested)
`IPV6=1, LAN=2 (detection logic, not a Phase H weapon), UPNP=3,
NATPMP=4, PCP=5, HOLE=6, PREDICT=7, MIRROR=8, RELAY=9, UPGRADE=10,
BIRTHDAY=11 (last on purpose)`.

### weapon_ops.h contract
Plain function per weapon (`xury_weapon_<name>_try(ctx, out)`), no
vtable. `XURY_ERR_INVAL` for programming errors only (NULL, bad
family). `XURY_OK + out->success=false` for real-world outcomes
(timeout, no response) — never a fabricated error code for an
expected network condition. `elapsed_ms` always recorded on
`XURY_OK`.

**Exception:** `scan/probing.c` does NOT follow this convention —
it follows `scan.c`'s own architecture rule instead ("every
sub-phase always records its own status in the result struct,"
because `scan.c` discards probing's return code via `(void)` cast).
So probing.c returns a real error on bind/setup failure AND still
sets `out->status` — different callers, different contracts,
documented explicitly in probing.c to prevent a future "fix" that
breaks scan.c.

### Blitz / Adaptive relationship
BLITZ is commander, not a weapon. ADAPTIVE is an optional advisor,
never a parent — BLITZ can run with zero adaptive input, can ignore
its suggestion, and ADAPTIVE must never call into or require BLITZ.
V1 = sequential, state-aware, bounded history (64), strict
loop/revisit detection. V2 (true concurrent racing + cancellation)
is deferred — needs engine-level lifecycle design, same category as
relay.c.

### Known bugs fixed this session (don't reintroduce)
- `probing.c`/`ipv6.c` wildcard bind used empty string — `inet_pton`
  rejects this. Fixed to literal `"0.0.0.0"` / `"::"`.
- `classify.c`'s original slope-based step detection was fragile to
  single-outlier repeats (see F.3 decisions above).
- `early_term.c` Rule 3 (CACHED_IPV6) had `rule_ipv6_global()` as a
  precondition, making it dead code (Rule 1 always fired first).
  Fixed: Rule 3 fires only when probing hasn't produced a result yet
  (`probing_has_data(p) == false`), not when probing explicitly
  confirmed or denied peer IPv6 support.

